{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdHfE5TLpDjMJyrVNkE7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Connect flow works again in a local test with a stubbed Intervals token endpoint. I haven't tried it against the real Intervals.icu. There were three separate causes, and each one alone was enough to break the connection. The full write-up is in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n1. **The Connect button always got \"unauthorized\".** The app's sign-in token is only sent on its own background API requests. The button was a plain link, and clicking a link sends no token, so the server rejected everyone. Now the server returns the Intervals authorize link as data, and the page fetches it with the token and then sends the browser there (`server.py`, `static/app.js`).\n2. **Intervals rejected the authorize step with 400 since the calendar sync shipped.** The requested permissions were `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,…,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`, which lists some areas twice. Intervals accepts one level per area, and write already includes read. Rivera's working grant in September followed that pattern. Changed to `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` in `config.py`.\n3. **Rivera was told to reconnect after about an hour.** Intervals access tokens don't expire, and the token response has no expiry time and no refresh token. The code assumed a 1-hour lifetime anyway, then threw away the still-valid token because it had no refresh token. Now an expiry is set only if Intervals sends one. A connection is marked \"expired, reconnect\" only when Intervals actually rejects the token, which is what happens if the athlete revokes access.\n\n**Still needed:**\n- **Reconnects:** Rivera has to connect once more, because their stored token was discarded and their old grant has no calendar permission. The same goes for anyone else connected before the calendar change who needs calendar sync.\n- **Exposed secrets:** `README.md` has an Intervals client secret in plain text, and `config.py` has a hard-coded fallback one. Both are committed. They should be rotated and read only from environment variables. I didn't change those files or contact any outside service.\n\nNothing is committed yet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1484,"cache_read_input_tokens":33760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1484},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0f382773-2acc-4e5c-996d-3b2b661bde62","uuid":"b093a2b0-e36f-4232-b319-5cec9f06ce5e","timestamp":"2026-11-02T02:09:51.546Z","request_id":"req_011CgdHfDpqQpmhNZYxXbBs6"}
